# Response headers for _headers-aware hosts (Cloudflare Pages / Netlify).
# Ignored by hosts that don't read this file (GitHub Pages, Vercel — Vercel needs
# the same rules restated in `vercel.json`). Copied verbatim into `dist/` by the
# build; the host consumes it and never serves it as a page.
# The reasoning for every rule below is in docs/deployment.md §6.

# --- Site-wide security headers -------------------------------------------------
# Permissions-Policy: the site calls none of these APIs, so deny them outright
# rather than trust that no future page starts calling one by accident.
#
# NO Content-Security-Policy here, deliberately: BaseLayout ships a pre-paint
# inline theme script and an inline <style> inside <noscript>, so a strict policy
# would need a per-build hash list and a static host cannot mint a nonce.
# 'unsafe-inline' would be a CSP that permits exactly the thing it exists to stop.
#
# NO Cache-Control on this rule, also deliberately: a catch-all would match
# /_astro/* too, and hosts differ in how they resolve two matching rules that both
# set the same header — the failure mode (a merged `max-age=0` winning over the
# immutable rule below) silently defeats asset caching. Per-path rules keep the
# question from arising.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()

# --- Content-hashed assets ------------------------------------------------------
# Astro fingerprints everything it emits here (e.g. ArrayRenderer.zPEiTlER.js), so
# a given filename's bytes never change — safe to cache forever. HTML is
# intentionally left on the host default so it revalidates and picks up the new
# hashes on each deploy.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# --- Unhashed, path-stable assets -----------------------------------------------
# These four are referenced by FIXED paths — three from every page's <head>, and
# og-default.png by link-preview scrapers — so their names survive a rebrand.
# That is exactly why they must not get the immutable rule above: replacing an
# icon or the OG card would otherwise keep serving the old bytes from every
# visitor's cache indefinitely. An hour of freshness, then revalidate (cheap: the
# host's ETag makes the revalidation a 304).
/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/favicon-32.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/apple-touch-icon.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/og-default.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate
